      "title": "Geometry: The Pythagorean Theorem",
      "renderer": "manim",
      "narration": "Our final topic in geometry focuses on a specific type of triangle that is incredibly important in mathematics and construction: the right-angled triangle. A right-angled triangle is any triangle that has one angle measuring exactly 90 degrees. The relationship between the lengths of the three sides of a right triangle is described by one of the most famous theorems in all of mathematics: the Pythagorean theorem. The theorem states that for any right-angled triangle, the square of the hypotenuse is equal to the sum of the squares of the other two sides. Let's define those terms. The two sides that form the 90-degree angle are called the 'legs', which we can label 'a' and 'b'. The side opposite the right angle, which is always the longest side, is called the 'hypotenuse', labeled 'c'. So, the formula is written as 'a squared plus b squared equals c squared'. This means if you draw a square on side 'a' and another square on side 'b', the combined area of those two squares will be exactly equal to the area of a square drawn on the hypotenuse, 'c'. This powerful and timeless theorem allows us to find the length of a missing side in a right triangle if we know the lengths of the other two.",
